  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...  \n\nHigh pressure and a cool air mass continues to build across the\nforecast area today. The result will be sunny skies but\ncontinued breezy weather. Forecast soundings support wind gusts\nof 20 to 30 MPH today, but the trend should be downward during\nthe afternoon as the cold air advection wanes. Highs will only\nbe in the 40s in the mountains today, with 50s elsewhere.\n\nWinds becoming light along with clear to mostly clear skies will\nsupport strong radiational cooling. Widespread frost is likely,\neven toward the coast. Therefore, we have hoisted a frost\nadvisory for the zones that remain active this season (the\nseason ends Oct 11). The exception is for the NH Seacoast where\ntemperatures may stay up a little bit more. Some areas in the\nfrost advisory may need to be upgraded to a freeze warning\ntoday.\n\nOtherwise, a warming trend begins Wed and peaks Thu. Highs by\nThu should be well into the 60s, with 70s at lower elevations. A\ncold front looks to move through Thursday with scattered\nshowers.\n\nThe surface trough moves off to the east Friday, but continued \nupper level troughing is going to provide the forcing for \nupslope showers in the mountains through Saturday morning before\nhigh pressure crests over the region by the afternoon. 850 mb \ntemperatures in the 0 to +3 C range will allow for seasonable \nafternoon temperatures in the 50s and 60s each day. High \npressure moves off to the east Sunday, and with continued \nridging aloft, dry weather should continue through at least the \ndaylight hours. Southwesterly flow brings 850 mb temperatures up\naround +10 C so Sundays highs likely mix into the 60s areawide.\nSignificant uncertainty is then introduced heading into early \nnext week as global models have a trough swinging out of the \nGreat Lakes Region and generating an area of low pressure of the\nMid-Atlantic, but at different times and intensities. The 19Z \nNBM suggests this would be the next chance of meaningful \nprecipitation with around 50% probabilities areawide of greater \nthan a tenth of an inch of rainfall in the Monday-Tuesday \nwindow. This will be a period to watch to see how models trend.",
